raw:
{
"slots": {
"question": [
{
"claim": "The paper investigates how the regional political climate moderates the relationship between an individual's self-reported political conservatism and their hesitancy toward or uptake of the COVID-19 vaccine and booster.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "The goal of the present research was to examine how political climate moderates the relationship between self-reported political conservatism and COVID-19 vaccine hesitancy and uptake.",
"page": 1
}
]
}
],
"central_claim": [
{
"claim": "The regional political climate significantly moderates the association between political conservatism and vaccine attitudes/behaviors, such that conservatives living in liberal climates are less hesitant and more likely to get boosters than conservatives in conservative climates, while liberals remain largely unaffected by their regional climate.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Controlling for age, education, income, and race, there was an interaction between political conservatism and conservative political climate for both vaccine and booster hesitancy... such that liberals were less likely to be hesitant regardless of political climate. However, conservatives living in liberal political climates were less vaccine hesitant than their conservative counterparts living in conservative regions.",
"page": 1
},
{
"text": "The interaction for self-reported booster status was consistent with those for vaccine and booster hesitancy... as political conservatism increased, the likelihood of receiving a booster was lower for participants in a conservative regional climate as compared to those in a liberal regional climate.",
"page": 10
}
]
}
],
"evidence": [
{
"claim": "The study utilized an online survey of 683 US participants collected in early 2023, measuring political conservatism, regional political climate (via 2020 presidential election vote share by zip code), and vaccine/booster status and hesitancy.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "We collected online survey data from 683 participants between March 8 and April 19, 2023... We assessed regional political climate using zip code data provided by participants and 2020 presidential election results sourced from the New York Times... We used this % Republican vote by zip code as a measure of conservative regional climate.",
"page": 3
},
{
"text": "Self-reported vaccine status and booster status were assessed through individual items... Vaccine hesitancy... was measured using the seven items from the Attitudes towards Adult Vaccination Scale... Booster hesitancy was assessed using items from a 2022 survey on COVID-19 booster hesitancy.",
"page": 3
}
]
}
],
"key_findings": [
{
"claim": "Political conservatism and conservative regional climate are both positively associated with vaccine and booster hesitancy, and negatively associated with vaccine and booster uptake.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Political conservatism and conservative regional climate were positively associated with vaccine hesitancy (β = .23, p < .001; β = .11, p = .001; respectively)... Additionally, the interaction between political conservatism and conservative regional climate was significant (β = .09, p = .003).",
"page": 8
},
{
"text": "Political conservatism and conservative regional climate were negatively associated with booster status (OR =.82, p = .022; OR =.77, p = .004; respectively). Additionally, there was a significant interaction... between political conservatism and conservative regional climate (OR =.83, p = .026).",
"page": 8
}
]
},
{
"claim": "Liberals exhibit low vaccine hesitancy and high booster uptake rates regardless of the political climate of their region.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "The most strikingly, for booster status the interaction between political orientation and political climate indicated that the most liberal participants were over 65% likely to get boosted regardless of climate.",
"page": 8
},
{
"text": "There was no difference in the likelihood of reporting receiving a booster based on conservative regional climate for the most politically liberal participants...",
"page": 10
}
]
},
{
"claim": "Conservatives living in liberal political climates have lower vaccine hesitancy and higher booster uptake rates than conservatives living in conservative political climates.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Conservatives living in liberal political climates were less vaccine hesitant than their conservative counterparts living in conservative regions.",
"page": 1
},
{
"text": "The most conservative participants in liberal climates were 60% likely to get boosted, while conservatives in conservative climates were less than 35% likely to get boosted.",
"page": 8
}
]
},
{
"claim": "The interaction between political conservatism and regional climate was not significant for initial vaccine status, unlike for hesitancy and booster status.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "The interaction between political conservatism and conservative regional climate (Table 6) was not significant (OR = 1.02, p = .833) [for vaccine status].",
"page": 8
},
{
"text": "The interaction for vaccination was not significant, this difference between attitudes (hesitancy) and action (vaccination), likely reflects the high prevalence of COVID-19 vaccine mandates.",
"page": 10
}
]
}
],
"scope_and_limits": [
{
"claim": "The study cannot establish causal or directional associations due to its cross-sectional design, and the use of zip code election data may be confounded by other regional factors like population density.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Finally, it should be noted that this data is cross-sectional and we cannot assume causal or directional associations.",
"page": 10
},
{
"text": "Because we used 2020 presidential election votes by zip code as a proxy for political climate, it must be noted that other zip code-based differences could be driving the associations noted in this paper. Regional factors (e.g., population density) may play a role...",
"page": 10
}
]
},
{
"claim": "The findings may not generalize to the broader US population because the sample was limited to White and Black participants recruited via an online panel, and relies on self-reported data susceptible to bias.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "Additionally, our sample was recruited through an online platform and only includes participants with access to this platform. Further, our sample includes only White and Black participants recruited through Qualtrics Panels, and may not generalize to broader populations in the United States.",
"page": 10
},
{
"text": "Another limitation in this dataset is the reliance on self-reported vaccine status. Self-report is susceptible to social desirability [30] and recall bias [31], and we cannot assume that reported vaccine status reflects actual vaccine status.",
"page": 10
}
]
}
],
"theoretical_positioning": [
{
"claim": "The authors extend the existing literature by combining the previously separate lines of research on individual political orientation and regional political climate to examine their interaction.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "While previous research has identified the unique impact of both political orientation and political climate on vaccination attitudes, there has been little research on the interaction between personal political orientation and local political climate.",
"page": 2
},
{
"text": "By looking at political orientation and region climate, we identify a novel and important finding, specifically that conservative individuals are more likely to received booster doses in liberal regions.",
"page": 10
}
]
}
],
"interlocutor_claim": [
{
"claim": "The paper disputes the notion that individual political orientation is the sole or primary predictor of vaccine hesitancy by showing that the regional context significantly alters the behavior of conservative individuals, suggesting that environmental factors play a crucial role.",
"quotes": [
{
"text": "The influence of political orientation on vaccine hesitancy has been increasing since the onset of the pandemic and political orientation has become a better predictor of vaccine hesitancy than demographic variables, pandemic-related fear, and trust in health institutions [18].",
"page": 2
},
{
"text": "These findings suggest that environmental factors (e.g., social pressure) may impact decisions to receive annual COVID-19 doses.",
"page": 10
}
]
}
]
}
}
